---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import { deviations, standardPhrases } from "../data/phraseology";
import { siteConfig } from "../site-config";

const standardCount = standardPhrases.length;
const deviationCount = deviations.length;
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Glossary"
  description="The phrases this course holds you to, and the forms it teaches you to hear as wrong."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p>
    Two lists. The first is the language: {standardCount} phrases, each with one
    meaning. The second is {deviationCount} forms you will hear constantly and
    should be able to name as departures.
  </p>

  <p>
    This page is the authority for the <em>standard phraseology</em> criterion
    on the <a href={withBase("/assessments/readback-drills/")}>readback drills</a>
    and for step two of the method in
    <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-11/")}>week 11</a> — name
    the standard form, or admit you do not have one.
  </p>

  <p class="glossary-note">
    It is also the list that <code>spec/phraseology.test.ts</code> checks the
    site's own transmissions against, so a transmission printed anywhere here
    as a model cannot contain a form from the second table. That check reads
    this page's source data, not a copy of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="standard">The phrases</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <caption>Standard phraseology, with the week that introduces each</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Phrase</th>
          <th scope="col">Means</th>
          <th scope="col">Week</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          standardPhrases.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>{p.phrase}</code></th>
              <td>
                {p.meaning}
                {p.note && <span class="phrase-note">{p.note}</span>}
              </td>
              <td class="week-cell">{p.week ?? "—"}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="deviations">The forms this course teaches you to hear as wrong</h2>

  <p>
    None of these is a mistake in English. Every one of them is fluent, and
    most of them are friendly — which is <a
      href={withBase("/lectures/week-07/")}>week 7</a
    >'s whole argument. Fluency is not compliance.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <caption>Known deviations, what to say instead, and the failure each invites</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Form</th>
          <th scope="col">Say instead</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
          <th scope="col">Week</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          deviations.map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>{d.form}</code></th>
              <td>{d.standard}</td>
              <td>{d.why}</td>
              <td class="week-cell">{d.week}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits">What this list cannot tell you</h2>

  <p>
    The second table is a <strong>blocklist</strong>, and that is a real limit
    rather than a rough edge. A transmission contains callsigns, place names,
    levels and squawks that no glossary could enumerate, so this list can prove
    a known error is present and can never prove a transmission is fully
    standard. The check that reads it is named for what it actually does.
  </p>

  <p>
    Which means: finding nothing here does not make a transmission correct. It
    makes it <em>not wrong in any of the {deviationCount} ways this course has
    taught you</em>. Week 11's method exists because the remaining judgement is
    yours.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .glossary-note {
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block: 1.5rem;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  caption {
    margin-block-end: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    text-align: start;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-bg-alt);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  thead th {
    border-block-end-width: 2px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  tbody th {
    font-weight: 400;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .week-cell {
    text-align: end;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .phrase-note {
    display: block;
    margin-block-start: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
